# the argument of hume-induction.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Hume's argument: no reasoning, demonstrative or probable, grounds trust in past experience.
# The supposition that the future will be like the past is called "uniformity" below.
atom	just_arg	we are engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement
atom	dem_arg	there is a demonstrative argument (concerning relations of ideas) for uniformity
atom	prob_arg	there is a probable argument (concerning matter of fact and existence) for uniformity
atom	conceivable	that the course of nature may change can be clearly and distinctly conceived
atom	contra	that the course of nature may change implies a contradiction
atom	founded_cause	all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect
atom	from_experience	our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience
atom	presuppose	all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past
atom	circular	proving uniformity by probable arguments is going in a circle, taking for granted the point in question
P1	S1	just_arg → dem_arg ∨ prob_arg	kind conceptual
# S3 and S4 are rhetorical questions asserting that a change in nature is conceivable.
P2	S3	conceivable
P3	S5	conceivable → ¬contra	kind conceptual
# S2's "since" makes the absence of contradiction the ground for there being no demonstrative argument.
P4	S2	¬contra → ¬dem_arg	kind conceptual
C1	S2	¬dem_arg	from P2 P3 P4
P5	S8	founded_cause ∧ from_experience ∧ presuppose
P6	S9	founded_cause ∧ from_experience ∧ presuppose → circular
A1	-	circular → ¬prob_arg
C2	S9	¬prob_arg	from P5 P6 A1
# S6 and S7 together say that no argument engages us to trust experience.
C	S7	¬just_arg	from P1 C1 C2
